<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Waithali</h1><p class="st-meta">370 CE – 818 CE (approx.) · capital Waithali</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second great kingdom of Arakan, whose Chandra kings left Sanskrit inscriptions and coins showing a Buddhist realm linked to Bengal and India.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rakhine chronicles say the capital moved from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dhanyawadi">Dhanyawadi</a> to Waithali, a new city a short distance to the south, around 370 under King Mahataing Chandra. Its walled site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyauktaw">Kyauktaw</a> has been excavated, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandachandra-inscription">Anandachandra inscription</a> of the early 8th century lists the kings of the dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Waithali's rulers, known as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chandra-dynasty">Chandra dynasty</a>, used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Indian royal styles and issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins bearing the conch and the trident-like srivatsa symbol. The kingdom traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> and patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> elements. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anandachandra-inscription">Anandachandra inscription</a> records the king's donations to monks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The chronicles say Waithali declined in the 8th and 9th centuries, and in 818 power passed to a new line in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemro-river">Lemro</a> valley, opening the Lemro period of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Kingdom of Arakan</a>. Waithali's ruins, with their brick walls and a large seated Buddha, survive near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyauktaw">Kyauktaw</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 370 CE – 818 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:67.06%;width:8.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>370 CE – 818 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Waithali</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahataing Chandra, Anandachandra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit (inscriptions)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Waithali, Vesali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=594&amp;focus=kingdom-of-waithali" aria-label="Kingdom of Waithali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7189 1416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Waithali lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7509 1672L7509 1672M7509 1672L7511 1673"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7509 1672h0M7511 1673h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7509 1672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7509 1672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Waithali. <a href="/atlas?year=594&amp;focus=kingdom-of-waithali">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
